\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{Rd}

%\VignetteEngine{knitr::knitr}
%\VignetteIndexEntry{Prime Number Sieve}
%\VignettePackage{numTheory}
%\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}

\title{Prime Number Generation using prime\_sieve}
\author{Siddhesh Akole}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette demonstrates the \texttt{prime\_sieve} function from the numTheory package.
The function generates all prime numbers up to a given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes,
an efficient algorithm for prime number generation.

\section{Inputs}
The function takes one integer input:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{n}: the upper bound up to which prime numbers are generated.
\end{itemize}

\section{Example}
Here we generate prime numbers up to 30:

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
library(numTheory)

prime_sieve(30)
@

\section{Constraints}
In R, exact integer range is: \texttt{- .Machine\$integer.max} to \texttt{.Machine\$integer.max}.  
For doubles, the representable range is: \texttt{.Machine\$double.xmin} to \texttt{.Machine\$double.xmax}.  

\section{Edge Cases}
\begin{itemize}
\item Edge case1: \texttt{n < 2}: returns an empty vector (no primes below 2).
\item Edge case2: \texttt{n = 2}: returns \texttt{2} as the only prime.
\item Edge case3: Large \texttt{n}: performance depends on memory, but algorithm remains efficient.
\end{itemize}

<<echo=TRUE, results='verbatim'>>=
# Edge case: n < 2
prime_sieve(1)

# Edge case: n = 2
prime_sieve(2)
@

\section{Complexity}
The \texttt{prime\_sieve} function uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which runs in 
\(\mathcal{O}(n \log \log n)\) time, making it efficient for generating primes 
up to large limits.

\section{Summary}
The \texttt{prime\_sieve} function provides a fast and reliable way to generate prime numbers,
useful for number theory, cryptography, and algorithmic applications.

\end{document}
